 """
+PARADOX EXPERIMENT -- twisted boundary conditions.  THIS FILE IS A COPY.
+
+It is learn/stage08_own_code/train_moire.py (repository commit b6f773f) with ONE
+addition, the option --twist T1 T2.  The original in learn/ is untouched;
+../CHANGES.diff lists every changed line.  Without --twist (or with
+--twist 0 0) this file computes exactly what the original computes
+(test_twist.py checks it).  Run it from this folder; launch_twisted.sh holds the
+command of the experiment.  The text from "Stage 8" on is the original's.
+
+What the twist is.  The periodic wave function returns to itself when one
+electron goes once around the supercell.  With a twist it returns multiplied by
+a fixed phase:
+    Psi(..., r_j + L_i, ...) = exp(2 pi i T_i) Psi(..., r_j, ...)      i = 1, 2
+T_1, T_2 are the twists along the two supercell vectors, in units of a full
+turn (--twist 1/3 1/3 is a third of a turn along each).  One line does it:
+    Psi_twisted(R) = exp(i k_twist . (r_1 + ... + r_N)) Psi_periodic(R)
+with k_twist = T_1 G_1 + T_2 G_2 (G_1, G_2: the supercell's reciprocal vectors,
+G_i . L_j = 2 pi if i = j, else 0).  Only the PHASE of Psi changes, so the
+sampling (|Psi|^2 at fixed weights) is untouched; the kinetic energy changes
+because it differentiates the phase.  Why: the one-electron momenta move from
+k to k + k_twist; at (1/3, 1/3) the 18 lowest levels are a closed shell
+(twist_shells.py), which they are not without a twist.
+A twisted wave function is necessarily complex: use --gradient full.
+
 Stage 8 -- train one network on the paper's moire system, with OUR code.
 
